                <h3>Terminal Architecture:</h3>
                <p>Barajas operates through a complex of interconnected facilities.</p>
                <h4>Terminal 1</h4>
                <p>Handles Schengen and some non-Schengen flights primarily.</p>
                <h4>Terminal 2</h4>
                <p>Serves European and domestic routes.</p>
                <h4>Terminal 3</h4>
                <p>Auxiliary facility connected to Terminal 2.</p>
                <h4>Terminal 4</h4>
                <p>The crown jewel of Barajas, inaugurated in 2006 and celebrated for its architectural brilliance. Designed by Antonio Lamela and Richard Rogers, </p>
                <p><b>Terminal 4 features:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Undulating bamboo ceilings with natural light flooding through</li>
                    <li>One of the world's largest terminal areas</li>
                    <li>Streamlined passenger flow, minimizing walking distances</li>
                    <li>Extensive retail and dining options</li>
                </ul>
                <h4>Terminal 4S (Satellite)</h4>
                <p>Dedicated to non-Schengen international flights, including all Miami arrivals.</p>
                <p><b>Connection details:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Located 2 km from Terminal 4</li>
                    <li>Linked by an automated underground people mover</li>
                    <li>Iberia and American arrivals are typically processed here</li>
                    <li>Air Europa arrivals typically at Terminal 1</li>
                </ul>
                <h4>Immigration and Customs at Barajas:</h4>
                <p>Passengers arriving from Miami clear Spanish entry formalities.</p>
                <p><b>Required documentation:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Valid passport with at least three months' validity beyond the planned departure date</li>
                    <li>No visa is required for US citizens staying under 90 days within the Schengen area</li>
                    <li>Return or onward travel documentation</li>
                    <li>Accommodation details if requested</li>
                </ul>
                US citizens enjoy visa-free entry for tourism, business, or family visits up to 90 days within any 180-day Schengen period.
