                <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                <h3>Q1: How long is the nonstop flight from Miami to Madrid?</h3>
                <p>The nonstop journey spans approximately 8 hours 25 minutes to 8 hours 50 minutes, depending on aircraft type and winds. Eastbound flights typically run shorter than westbound returns due to prevailing jet stream assistance.</p>

                <h3>Q2: Which airlines fly nonstop between Miami and Madrid?</h3>
                <p>Three carriers operate nonstop service: Iberia, American Airlines, and Air Europa. All depart from Miami International and arrive at Madrid-Barajas. Multiple daily departures provide scheduling flexibility across all three airlines.</p>

                <h3>Q3: Do US citizens need a visa for Spain?</h3>
                <p>No visas are required for tourism or business stays under 90 days within any 180-day Schengen period. A valid passport with at least three months' validity beyond departure suffices. No additional documentation is needed for standard visits.</p>

                <h3>Q4: What is the cheapest month to fly from Miami to Madrid?</h3>
                <p>November and January historically offer the lowest fares, with economy round-trips sometimes available below $450. August commands premium pricing. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend travel by significant margins.</p>

                <h3>Q5: How do I get from Madrid airport to the city center?</h3>
                <p>Metro Line 8 connects all terminals to the city for approximately €5. The Airport Express Bus runs 24 hours to key points for €5. Taxis charge a fixed €30 airport rate to central areas. Cercanías commuter rail serves Terminal 4 and major stations.</p>

                <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Madrid?</h3>
                <p>The Euro is Spain's currency. US dollars are not accepted. Credit cards work universally at hotels, restaurants, and shops. ATMs plentiful throughout the city. Notify your bank before traveling to prevent card blocks.</p>

                <h3>Q7: What terminal do Miami flights arrive at in Madrid?</h3>
                <p>Iberia and American flights typically arrive at Terminal 4S, the satellite facility for non-Schengen international flights. Air Europa usually arrives at Terminal 1. An automated people mover connects T4S to the main terminal complex.</p>

                <h3>Q8: Is Madrid safe for American tourists?</h3>
                <p>Madrid ranks among Europe's safest major cities. Standard urban precautions apply regarding pickpockets in tourist zones and on the metro. Violent crime against tourists is rare. Police presence is visible and helpful. The emergency number is 112.</p>

                <h3>Q9: What should I pack for Madrid?</h3>
                <p>Comfortable walking shoes essential for cobblestone streets and museum marathons. Layers accommodate temperature swings between warm days and cool evenings. Smart casual attire for evening dining. Universal power adapter for European outlets. Sun protection for summer visits.</p>

                <h3>Q10: How early should I arrive at Miami airport for Madrid flights?</h3>
                <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from Miami. Security and immigration queues can be unpredictable, particularly during peak morning and evening periods. Check-in closes 60 minutes before departure on most carriers.</p>














